WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:09.413
There were frightening moments during Roald Amundsen’s expedition to conquer the South Pole that tested the character of each and every man with him.

2
00:00:09.414 --> 00:00:22.005
During one leg of the journey, Amundsen recalled, "We had to work our way across a hard, smooth slope, which formed an angle of 45 degrees, and ended in a huge bottomless chasm . . .

3
00:00:22.006 --> 00:00:28.775
The prospect of seeing sledge, driver, and dogs slide down sideways and disappear into the abyss was a great one."

4
00:00:28.776 --> 00:00:37.888
But Roald Amundsen had prepared well, so he began his expedition hoping the obstacles nature put before him would not defeat him.

5
00:00:37.889 --> 00:00:55.595
Born in Borge, Norway, on July 16, 1872, Roald Amundsen decided to be a polar explorer at age 15 after reading about Sir John Franklin, the famous British Arctic explorer who died in 1847 when his final expedition met with disaster.

6
00:00:55.596 --> 00:01:04.804
Amundsen was attracted by Franklin’s adventurous life, but also felt that the doomed expedition was mostly the explorer’s own fault.

7
00:01:04.805 --> 00:01:13.637
Amundsen believed he could do better at conquering the unknown, so at age 21, he went to sea to gain command experience.

8
00:01:13.638 --> 00:01:22.281
In 1897, he obtained a position as first mate on the Belgica , a ship commissioned to conduct research in

9
00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:32.235
Antarctica. When the Belgica became frozen in the ice during the trip, Amundsen experienced the brutal hardships a ship’s crew can suffer because of an unprepared

10
00:01:32.236 --> 00:01:41.101
captain. In addition to choosing a crew of mostly incompetent and lazy men, the captain hadn’t even brought enough food or warm clothing for an extended

11
00:01:41.102 --> 00:01:49.707
voyage. Then, while facing starvation, the crew was brought to near insanity by a lack of activity through the endless Antarctic night of the winter

12
00:01:49.708 --> 00:01:56.540
months. It was only when the captain fell ill and the ship’s doctor took command that the expedition was

13
00:01:56.541 --> 00:02:04.079
saved. In 1903, Amundsen planned and led the first expedition to obtain scientific data on the magnetic north pole.

14
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:18.088
He also wanted to find, and became the first to navigate, the elusive Northwest Passage—a possible shipping route many believed to exist north of Canada that would allow vessels to reach the Pacific Ocean from the Northern Atlantic Ocean.

15
00:02:18.089 --> 00:02:23.969
This unknown water route had been sought by navigators for centuries, but had never been discovered.

16
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:32.678
On this expedition, Amundsen traveled throughout the area meeting the native people, and he learned from the Inuit how to survive in the bitter cold.

17
00:02:32.679 --> 00:02:38.836
This knowledge became invaluable when Amundsen’s ship became locked in ice for three years.

18
00:02:38.837 --> 00:02:47.045
The Inuit taught him how to handle a dogsled, and Amundsen learned that animal skins kept him warmer than wool coats.

19
00:02:47.046 --> 00:02:59.106
In September of 1909, Amundsen was in the process of planning another expedition to the North Pole when he heard that the American explorer Robert Peary had already accomplished that feat.

20
00:02:59.107 --> 00:03:06.529
Immediately, Amundsen decided to challenge the British explorer Robert Scott, who was about to try for the South Pole.

21
00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.854
But Amundsen kept his decision secret.

22
00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:17.491
He was afraid his financial backers would want to see if Scott was successful before committing money to a similar expedition.

23
00:03:17.492 --> 00:03:24.171
So Amundsen didn’t even tell his crew about his change of plans until his expedition was well under way.

24
00:03:24.172 --> 00:03:32.958
To increase his odds of success, Amundsen tried to anticipate every difficulty that might arise, and made plans to avoid or overcome them.

25
00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:41.958
He packed provisions for a much longer expedition than he expected, and assembled a crackerjack crew of eight mentally and physically strong men.

26
00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:50.572
For his ship, Amundsen chose the Fram , a unique vessel able to withstand the pressures of the ice that would inevitably surround

27
00:03:50.573 --> 00:03:58.611
it. Lastly, he chose 110 Greenland sled dogs, a breed accustomed to surviving in the frozen

28
00:03:58.612 --> 00:04:10.268
wilderness. On June 7, 1910, the Fram set off from Oslo, Norway, and reached the Ross Ice Shelf on January 14, 1911—significantly ahead of Robert

29
00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:25.465
Scott. The crew built Framheim, a base camp of tents and snow buildings three kilometers from shore, and during the next three months, they transported supplies to three depots about one hundred kilometers apart along the route to the South

30
00:04:25.466 --> 00:04:34.357
Pole. On April 22, when the sun was visible for the last time until spring, the men at Framheim settled into the long Antarctic

31
00:04:34.358 --> 00:04:44.573
winter. To help them cope with total darkness and temperatures that would reach as low as -55 °C, Amundsen planned definite routines for his

32
00:04:44.574 --> 00:04:54.719
crew. For the next four months, each crew member had chores: hunting seals, cleaning, cooking, charting scientific observations, fueling lamps and heaters, and caring for the

33
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.552
dogs. At night they played cards and darts and even did

34
00:04:58.553 --> 00:05:10.276
needlework. The sun finally rose again on August 24, and preparations resumed so that on October 19, 1911, Amundsen and four of his men finally set off for the South

35
00:05:10.277 --> 00:05:19.040
Pole. The other four remained behind to make scientific observations on King Edward VII Land, a peninsula on the edge of the Ross Ice

36
00:05:19.041 --> 00:05:33.231
Shelf. Amundsen’s group proceeded toward the pole with four sleds and fifty-two dogs; as temperatures ranged from -21 °C to -30 °C, the dogs pulled the sleds while the men skied

37
00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:39.969
alongside. The men and the sleds traveled approximately 17 kilometers a day on flat land and gentle

38
00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:45.453
rises. They stopped and built snow beacons from blocks of ice to guide them on the way

39
00:05:45.454 --> 00:05:55.469
back. The men’s food consisted of biscuits, chocolate and dried milk for hot chocolate, and pemmican—a substance made of dried meat, fat, and fruits used by the

40
00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:02.406
Inuit. Soon, however, they entered a landscape of rugged mountains, ice blocks, shallow cracks, and deep

41
00:06:02.407 --> 00:06:12.043
chasms. The dogs trudged up peaks and glaciers ranging from 610 to 3,375 meters high, pulling the sleds plus the men on skis.

42
00:06:12.044 --> 00:06:21.648
At times, the steepness of the slope was so great that the heavy sleds had to ascend one at a time with twice as many dogs pulling each sled to make it up the mountain.

43
00:06:21.649 --> 00:06:26.823
On the descent, ropes were wrapped around the sleds’ runners to slow them down.

44
00:06:26.824 --> 00:06:31.039
Blizzards delayed the group, making the journey more difficult.

45
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:43.369
But on December 14, 1911, after almost two months, all five explorers finally planted the Norwegian flag at the South Pole on a plateau more than 3,000 meters high.

46
00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:56.065
Leaving behind a small tent, a letter to the king of Norway, and a note to Robert Scott, the men set off three days later on the return trip with only two sleds, eating the food they had stored along the way.

47
00:06:56.066 --> 00:07:05.273
On January 25, 1912, the five men and their eleven surviving dogs reached Framheim, 99 days after they had started out.

48
00:07:05.274 --> 00:07:16.611
Robert Scott reached the pole about a month later; tragically, he had chosen a starting point farther from the pole than Amundsen and used ponies to carry supplies instead of hardy sled dogs.

49
00:07:16.612 --> 00:07:22.817
When his expedition experienced terrible weather on their return, Scott and his men froze to death.

50
00:07:22.818 --> 00:07:30.967
Roald Amundsen never stopped exploring, and in 1925, he took two planes as far north as anyone had ever flown.

51
00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:38.689
The next year, he flew over the North Pole in an airship and verified that the North Pole was made of nothing but ice.

52
00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:50.016
Unfortunately, however, on June 18, 1928, while attempting to rescue a group of lost explorers in the Arctic, Amundsen’s plane disappeared and he was never seen again.

53
00:07:50.017 --> 00:08:02.395
But his memory is kept alive by all the places that have been named for him: among those, the Amundsen Sea, the Amundsen Glacier, and at the moon’s south pole, the Amundsen Crater.

